"Trust but Verify: Designing Region-InhomogeneousForward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1)\nciteturn41261view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1\",\"lineno\":180}); Total lines: 288\nL161: #### Metrics.\nL162: Repair: detection precision/recall over planted positions, correction accuracy (edited to the ground-truth token), and the false-edit rate on clean tokens, our primary safety metric. Infill: token-F1 against ground truth, computed per hole span and averaged over all spans in a cell; deltas are reported in points ($\\times 100$) with paired-bootstrap 95% confidence intervals (10k resamples of passages, same statistic as the tables).\nL163: Passage quality: mean NLL of the completed passage under a gpt2-large judge (cite60†Radford et al., 2019 ), since exact match cannot credit valid-but-different repairs. Containment: infill token accuracy binned by distance to the nearest corrupted context token. Non-regression: clean-context ($\\rho=0$) metrics and the held-out variational bound relative to arm A.\nL164: ### 4.2 Results\nL165: Table 2: Headline cell: plausible corruptions, $\\rho=0.1$, 30% hole coverage ($n=256$, paired). Paired-bootstrap F1 deltas vs. A (points): DP $+0.51$ $[-0.26,+1.28]$; D $+0.38$ $[-0.36,+1.09]$; E $+0.51$ $[-0.30,+1.31]$; CP $+0.40$ $[-0.31,+1.10]$; none is significant.\nL166: Arm  | Infill F1  | Det. P  | Det. R  | False-edit %  | Correction  | Judge NLL $\\downarrow$\nL167: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL168: base  | 0.275  | –  | –  | –  | –  | 3.250\nL169: A  | 0.287  | –  | –  | –  | –  | 3.237\nL170: B  | 0.286  | 0.572  | 0.035  | 0.29  | 0.756  | 3.209\nL171: C  | 0.286  | 0.716  | 0.058  | 0.26  | 0.756  | 3.173\nL172: D  | 0.291  | 0.668  | 0.150  | 0.83  | 0.730  | 3.083\nL173: E  | 0.292  | 0.625  | 0.272  | 1.82  | 0.716  | 2.980\nL174: CP  | 0.291  | 0.878  | 0.197  | 0.31  | 0.773  | 3.052\nL175: DP  | 0.292  | 0.752  | 0.350  | 1.28  | 0.743  | 2.902\nL176: EXT  | 0.279  | 0.062  | 0.029  | 4.87  | 0.260  | 3.101\nL177: #### Repair (H1).\nL178: The training-time forward process appears necessary for the capability: the vanilla model with the same sampler (arm B) detects almost nothing (recall 0.035), because a model whose training dynamics never visit a wrong unmasked state assigns no meaningful likelihood to observed positions; the pretrained checkpoint gives $\\approx$50% probability to uniform-random garbage in context.\nL179: After the $\\eta$-finetune, conspicuous corruptions are caught nearly completely: at uniform $\\rho=0.1$, arm D reaches recall 0.959 $[0.954,0.963]$ at precision 0.909 with a 1.07% false-edit rate (DP: recall 0.959 at precision 0.853, 1.84% false-edit), and corrections land on the exact ground-truth token 52–77% of the time across $\\eta$-arms and corruption types.\nL180: On out-of-distribution plausible corruptions (Table cite63†2 ), the data-dependent kernel more than doubles recall over the uniform kernel (DP 0.350 vs. D 0.150) at precision 0.752, though with a higher false-edit rate (1.28% vs. 0.83%), consistent with training noise nearer the data conditional transferring better to corruptions drawn near it. Even so, two thirds of plausible corruptions go undetected. False-edit rates on fully clean context stay between 0.07% and 0.39% for the mixture-trained arms.\nL181: At $\\rho=0.2$ recall pins at $\\approx$0.76 across $\\eta$-arms; this is the $\\hat{\\rho}=0.15$ edit budget binding, not a detection ceiling.\nL182: #### Containment (H2).\nL183: Where corruption actually poisons infilling, the method removes the poison. Under uniform corruption at $\\rho=0.1$, vanilla arms show the contamination signature: infill accuracy drops to 0.088–0.11 in the 1–2-token bin adjacent to a corruption, below their own 0.14–0.16 at distance 3–4.\nL184: Every $\\eta$-arm inverts the dip (accuracy adjacent to a now-repaired corruption reaches 0.20–0.24, as high as anywhere in the curve), and all curves converge by distance $\\geq 5$: uniform contamination is local, and the method eliminates exactly that local damage (Figure cite64†2 ). Under plausible corruption, all arms coincide within noise at every distance. Locally plausible substitutions barely poison neighboring infills even for the vanilla model, so there is little to contain.\nL185: #### Aggregate infill quality.\nL186: The pre-registered primary gate, arm D over arm A by $\\geq 3$ F1 points at $\\rho=0.1$, passes decisively on uniform corruptions and fails on plausible ones, and we report both unedited. Uniform: D$-$A $=+3.84$ $[+2.82,+4.91]$ at $\\rho=0.1$ and $+6.42$ $[+5.42,+7.47]$ at $\\rho=0.2$. At $\\rho=0.1$, E ($+4.47$) and DP ($+3.64$) also clear the gate; C ($+3.15$ $[+2.25,+4.10]$) clears it narrowly, and CP ($+2.46$ $[+1.67,+3.29]$) improves significantly but falls short.\nL187: Plausible: D$-$A $=+0.38$ $[-0.36,+1.09]$; the best arms, DP and E, reach $+0.51$, small and not statistically significant. At $\\rho=0.2$ plausible, gains are larger and significant (D $+1.19$ $[+0.32,+2.06]$, DP $+1.69$ $[+0.72,+2.65]$) but still below the gate. We attribute the failure mainly to limited headroom: plausible corruptions cost the vanilla baseline only $\\approx$2.3 F1 points at $\\rho=0.1$, so three points of recovery are not available.\nL188: Low plausible-corruption recall ($\\leq 0.35$) also limits the gain, so headroom is not the only factor. The full $13$-cell grid is in Appendix cite34†A .\nL189: Table 3: Judge-LM passage quality: gpt2-large NLL of the completed passage, nats/token (lower is better), 30% hole coverage.\nL190: Arm  | Clean  | Uniform $\\rho{=}0.1$  | Plausible $\\rho{=}0.1$\nL191: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL192: base  | 2.583  | 4.120  | 3.250\nL193: A  | 2.565  | 4.084  | 3.237\nL194: B  | 2.578  | 4.014  | 3.209\nL195: C  | 2.585  | 2.746  | 3.173\nL196: D  | 2.581  | 2.610  | 3.083\nL197: E  | 2.593  | 2.626  | 2.980\nL198: CP  | 2.576  | 2.823  | 3.052\nL199: DP  | 2.573  | 2.610  | 2.902\nL200: #### Passage quality under the judge.\nL201: The judge LM measures what exact match cannot (Table cite65†3 ). Under uniform corruption, repair restores 97% of the corruption-induced damage: arm A pays $+1.52$ nats/token over its clean reference, arms D/DP pay $+0.03$–$0.04$. Under plausible corruption, DP scores 2.902 vs. 3.237 for A ($-0.335$ nats/token; clean reference 2.573). We read this as a passage-level improvement that token-F1 cannot credit, since repairs producing valid-but-different text count as errors under exact match.\nL202: Judge NLL under a single LM is an indirect proxy, however, and may also reward fluent but unfaithful repairs.\nL203: #### Non-regression of the learned reverse dynamics (H3).\nL204: On clean conditioning, paired F1 deltas between treated arms and A straddle zero, judge NLL is identical to within 0.03 nats, and the held-out variational bound moves by $+0.0036$ nats/token (A 3.4072 vs. C/D weights 3.4108; gate $\\leq 0.02$), with all finetunes improving on the base checkpoint by $\\approx$0.35 nats through FineWeb-Edu adaptation. Removing the $\\eta{=}0$ mixture (arm E) is the one configuration that pays: persistently elevated clean-context cross-entropy (0.06 vs.\nL205: 0.03 nats), the largest bound delta ($+0.0071$), and the highest false-edit rate at the headline cell (1.82%). It is a more aggressive editor, at a measurable cost in calibration on clean input. Arm E differs from D in two ways, however: it drops the $\\eta{=}0$ component and raises the mean context-corruption rate (0.15 vs. 0.0375), so the comparison does not isolate the mixture itself; a matched-mean control is future work.\nL206: We also did not train a spatially homogeneous hybrid-noise arm, so the contrast with reports that such noise pays a downstream cost (cite50†von Rütte et al., 2025 ) is indirect.\nL207: #### Annealing (H4).\nL208: Moving from frozen-context read-out repair to the annealed sampler on the same weights raises plausible-corruption detection recall from 0.058 to 0.150 (uniform-trained C$\\to$D) and from 0.197 to 0.350 (hard-negative-trained CP$\\to$DP), at higher false-edit rates (0.26$\\to$0.83% and 0.31$\\to$1.28%). This comparison changes several factors at once: edit timing, the threshold (annealed from 0.5 vs. fixed at 0.1), the edit budget, and refilling by iterative reverse steps rather than a single read-out.\nL209: It therefore supports the sampler as a whole, not annealing specifically; isolating the schedule would require, for example, a fixed-threshold early-edit arm.\nL210: #### External-detector baseline.\nL211: On uniform corruptions, GPT-2 surprisal flagging is competitive but consistently behind: recall 0.90 vs. arm D’s 0.96 at $\\rho=0.1$, F1 0.297 vs. 0.304, judge NLL 2.701 vs. 2.610, with the gap widening at $\\rho=0.2$, where EXT recovers $+4.9$ F1 points over A against arm D’s $+6.4$. Against this detector the integrated detect–remask–refill loop is ahead even on conspicuous noise, although EXT receives a per-sequence edit budget matched to DP’s and refills with the same class of model.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProgressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1)\nciteturn41261view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1\",\"lineno\":200}); Total lines: 448\nL190: This observation is supported by the fact that bigger models have stronger in-context capabilities, which self-distillation exploits (see Section 4.1 of cite77†Hübotter et al. (2026) ). Bigger models have a larger shift in their token distribution when provided with additional in-context cues. This shift then translates into a stronger signal, making progressive misalignment more prominent (cite24†Section 3.5 ).\nL191: ### 3.5 Further observations on progressive misalignment\nL192: \nL193: We discuss a possible explanation for why, in general, progressive misalignment might emerge under benign RSD. While this provides an initial understanding, a more thorough investigation should be explored in future work.\nL194: We start by recalling that, both with and without additional context, in all of our settings, the initial model refuses all prompts used during self-distillation (cite54†Appendix C ). In other words, the initial generations on which self-distillation is performed are entirely safe. It is thus surprising that recursively self-distilling the model on its own resampled generations progressively makes it unsafe more broadly.\nL195: We hypothesize that part of this safety failure is due to the small displacement in the token distribution induced by the additional cue added to the teacher’s context, which is then bootstrapped and amplified during recursive self-distillation. To investigate this hypothesis, we report in cite100†Figure 8b , at each epoch and token position, the mean $\\mathrm{KL}$ divergence between the initial and updated models, averaged over all generations from SORRY-Bench.\nL196: As training proceeds, we observe that the model progressively diverges from its initial policy. Moreover, such divergence is concentrated in the first few tokens of the answer. This behavior matches previous observations in the literature about the shallowness of safety training (cite101†Qi et al., 2025 ). Thus, a benign cue can be enough to change the first tokens’ distribution and, in turn, cause progressive misalignment.\nL197: We think that fully understanding which cues give rise to this effect is another exciting future direction to explore.\nL198: The shift in the distribution at initial token positions also suggests why using does not induce misalignment. Certain cues further incentivize the probability mass to concentrate on refusal tokens that had high probability under the initial model, avoiding the amplification of harmful effects described above. Additionally, we note that if the model is self-distilled for long enough, generations will eventually become degenerate (cite102†Figure 8a ), e.g., by echoing the feedback.\nL199: This is a well-known phenomenon of generative models trained recursively on their own outputs (cite103†Alemohammad et al., 2024 ); we show this happens in our experiments as well (cite36†Section A.2 ).\nL200: 8a Illustration of the effect of RSD in policy space. An initially safe model progressively becomes unsafe and compliant to harmful requests before collapsing.\nL201: \nL202: 8b KL between the initial model and the self-distilled one (with or without the cue) for each token position and epoch. Qwen3 14B, with\nL203: ## 4 Related Work\nL204: #### Breaking safety alignment.\nL205: A growing body of work has shown that safety alignment in LLMs can be substantially weakened by relatively little additional fine-tuning. cite67†Qi et al. (2024) demonstrate that adversarially chosen examples or fine-tuning on benign instruction data can weaken refusal behavior. cite95†He et al. (2024) identifies benign examples that break safety through representation similarity; cite104†Yong & Bach (2026) shows that benign reasoning training can lead to unsafe motivated reasoning. cite101†Qi et al.\nL206: (2025) argues that this brittleness stems from shallow safety alignment, where safety training primarily modifies the distribution over the first few generated tokens (see cite100†Figure 8b as well). Misalignment has also been observed to arise when optimizing models from in-context reward hacking (ICRH) (cite105†Pan et al., 2024a ; cite106†Pan et al., 2024b ) or optimizing user feedback via RLHF (cite107†Williams et al., 2025 ; cite108†Howe & Carroll, 2025 ).\nL207: Unlike these, we study a recursive misalignment failure where the model is continuously updated, unlike in ICRH where the model is frozen, and consider self-distillation instead of RLHF.\nL208: #### Distillation for Model Safety\nL209: Distillation has been investigated as a possible algorithmic solution to improve model safety. For example, cite109†Askell et al. (2021) does distillation of a helpful, honest, and harmless (HHH) prompt to embed these properties into the model. LLama 2 (cite110†Touvron et al., 2023 ) applied context-distillation in their safety post-training pipeline.\nL210: Self-distillation has also been used to improve safety alignment and personalization (cite111†Lee et al., 2026 ; cite112†Qin et al., 2026 ; cite91†Kleine Buening et al., 2026 ). Our work shows that when applied recursively and benignly, self-distillation can unintentionally degrade the safety alignment of these models instead of improving it.\nL211: #### Emergent Misalignment and Subliminal Learning.\nL212: cite113†Betley et al. (2025) showed that fine-tuning a model on a narrow set of insecure code induces broad misalignment across otherwise unrelated prompts, a phenomenon they call emergent misalignment. In their setup, the external training data contains the unsafe behavior; in progressive misalignment, the failure mode arises from initial benign data generated by the model itself. cite114†Cloud et al.\nL213: (2026) defines subliminal learning as the transmission of behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls) from a teacher to a student through seemingly unrelated data, such as sequences of numbers or code. cite68†Aden-Ali et al. (2026) show how unrelated subsets of a dataset can be systematically selected to induce such transmission. Subliminal learning requires a teacher that already exhibits the transmitted trait.\nL214: In progressive misalignment, by contrast, unsafe behavior appears only as the recursion proceeds and not from the beginning.\nL215: ## 5 Discussion\nL216: #### Implications for AI Safety.\nL217: One of the emerging paradigms in AI development is to have current models partake in their own improvement, a process referred to as recursive self-improvement (RSI) (cite115†Fang et al., 2025 ; cite116†Gao et al., 2025 ; cite61†Anthropic, 2026 ; cite117†Huang et al., 2026 ). Self-distillation can be viewed as one possible component of RSI, as it does not require human supervision.\nL218: Our results indicate that safety degradation can accumulate across successive rounds of self-improvement, without the initial model appearing unsafe. We believe that a mature understanding of AI alignment, which we do not currently possess, should predict surprising results like ours in advance and help us understand how different training regimes can erode the safety property of frontier AI systems.\nL219: #### Limitations and future work.\nL220: So far, we have validated progressive misalignment only on two model families, and up to a size of 32B. For broader conclusiveness, one could extend the study towards even larger models and other types of feedback or additional information. Further, we only have an initial hypothesis for the cause of progressive misalignment.\nL221: It would be interesting to investigate potential causes more systematically to answer, for example, the following questions: which properties of the feedback trigger the emergence of misalignment? Does misalignment progressively accumulate when self-distillation is applied recursively to improve downstream capabilities? For example, could it happen that when we select coding tasks that improve the model’s coding, safety starts eroding? Could a more effective data-selection approach than the one we propose (cite20†Section 3.3 ) serve as a possible mitigation? Another exciting direction is to study the emergence of progressive misalignment in agentic and recursively improving systems beyond self-distillation.\nL222: #### Conclusion.\nL223: \nL224: We show that aligned models can become misaligned when self-distilled starting from their own safe generations. Our findings further underline the pressing importance of better understanding the alignment of frontier AI systems; we are on the verge of handing off their development, and risks and unexpected behavior will only increase in quantity and magnitude.\nL225: ### Acknowledgments\nL226: \nL227: We would like to thank Sophie Bellini for help designing the illustrations in the paper. We are grateful for great discussions and feedback to, listed in no particular order, Joan Velja, Prasann Singhal, Yaowen Ye, Sarah Martinson, Annalisa Barbara, Itamar Pres, Ryan Wang, Mosh Levy, and Tianyi Alex Qiu. KAS is supported by a Cooperative AI PhD Fellowship. FDG is supported by the Swiss National Science Foundation (Grant number 218343).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRank Measures of Fine-Tuning UpdatesNeed a Noise Baseline (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1)\nciteturn41261view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1\",\"lineno\":225}); Total lines: 624\nL208: The pretrained weights are a harder reference than noise, since they are themselves far from random, and the updates are more concentrated than them too, on average. The stable rank of the pretrained weights is 0.178 on Qwen and 0.140 on SmolLM2, against 0.093–0.097 and 0.020–0.034 for the SFT, wikitext and code updates: 46–48% below the weights on Qwen and 75–86% on SmolLM2, with layer-bootstrap intervals well clear of zero.\nL209: This does not hold for every matrix type: on Qwen, for the down projection, the SFT update and the weights are level (Table cite62†11 ). The threshold count cannot see the difference either: it puts the Qwen updates and the pretrained weights at the same 97–98%.\nL210: ### 4.3 Instruction and preference training leave no special mark\nL211: Table 2: Differences in stable rank between runs on the same model, paired matrix by matrix, with 95% layer-bootstrap intervals. The intervals describe the spread over layers within one run, not between training runs; “second seed” repeats a run with a different seed and data sample. “Steps matched” trains on code for as many optimizer steps as SFT, which is 2.5–2.7 times more code tokens.\nL212: difference  | Qwen2.5-0.5B  | SmolLM2-360M\nL213: SFT $-$ wikitext  | $+0.004$ ($-0.001$, $+0.010$)  | $+0.005$ ($+0.003$, $+0.006$)\nL214: SFT $-$ code  | $+0.003$ ($-0.000$, $+0.006$)  | $-0.009$ ($-0.011$, $-0.008$)\nL215: code $-$ wikitext  | $+0.002$ ($-0.004$, $+0.007$)  | $+0.014$ ($+0.012$, $+0.016$)\nL216: shuffled words $-$ SFT  | $-0.054$ ($-0.062$, $-0.047$)  | $-0.016$ ($-0.018$, $-0.014$)\nL217: code, steps matched $-$ code  | $+0.020$ ($+0.018$, $+0.023$)  | $+0.007$ ($+0.007$, $+0.008$)\nL218: swapped DPO $-$ DPO  | $+0.002$ ($+0.000$, $+0.004$)  | –\nL219: second seed $-$ first, SFT  | $-0.001$ ($-0.005$, $+0.001$)  | $-0.000$ ($-0.001$, $-0.000$)\nL220: second seed $-$ first, wikitext  | $-0.001$ ($-0.005$, $+0.003$)  | $+0.000$ ($-0.000$, $+0.000$)\nL221: second seed $-$ first, code  | $-0.000$ ($-0.001$, $+0.001$)  | $-0.000$ ($-0.001$, $+0.000$)\nL222: second seed $-$ first, DPO  | $+0.001$ ($-0.001$, $+0.002$)  | –\nL223: A concentrated SFT update says nothing about instruction tuning unless fine-tuning on other data looks different. With the model, optimizer and number of training tokens held fixed, SFT does not stand out. On Qwen, SFT, wikitext and code give stable ranks of 0.097, 0.093 and 0.094; every paired difference between them is under 0.005 and its interval includes zero (Table cite86†2 ), and a second seed moves each by at most 0.0014.\nL224: On SmolLM2 the data does leave a mark: code is the least concentrated (0.034), wikitext the most (0.020) and SFT sits between them (0.025). These differences are real, not seed noise: a second seed of each SmolLM2 run moves its stable rank by at most 0.0003.\nL225: Nor are they an artefact of the code runs taking 2.6–2.7 times fewer optimizer steps than SFT (§cite14†3 ): training on code for as many steps as SFT, which is 2.5–2.7 times more code tokens, moves the code update further from SFT, not closer, on both models (stable rank 0.114 on Qwen, 0.042 on SmolLM2). So what the data teaches, and how much of it, does shape the update. What it does not do is single out instruction tuning: in both models SFT does not stand out from the ordinary language-model runs.\nL226: Similar stable rank means a similar spectral shape, not the same update, so we also compared the directions. For each pair of runs we measured the overlap of the top-16 left singular subspaces of each matrix’s update, averaged over matrices (1 would be identical subspaces; two random subspaces give 0.02–0.04; Appendix cite45†F ). Two runs on the same kind of data with different seeds overlap by 0.26–0.32 on Qwen and 0.34–0.51 on SmolLM2.\nL227: Runs on different data overlap less, 0.20–0.22 on Qwen and 0.12–0.13 on SmolLM2, but still four to six times more than random. So the data steers which directions an update uses, more than it changes the update’s shape.\nL228: Preference training gives the same answer. Starting from the Qwen SFT model, we ran DPO (cite87†Rafailov et al., 2023 ) on 10k UltraFeedback pairs (cite88†Cui et al., 2024 ), and a control on the same pairs with chosen and rejected swapped in a random half of them, in the spirit of random-label controls (cite89†Zhang et al., 2017 ).\nL229: The prompts, responses and amount of training are identical, but there is no net preference to learn; the control does share the unswapped half of its pairs with the DPO run, so it removes the preference signal, not everything the two runs see in common (Appendix cite40†A ). On about 190 held-out pairs the DPO model prefers the chosen answer 65% of the time (74% for a second seed); the control is at chance, 51%.\nL230: Yet the two updates are alike on every measure (Table cite80†1 ): stable rank 0.053 for DPO and 0.055 for the control, against 0.436 for noise, and the two updates have the same size (Table cite71†8 ). The paired difference, $+0.002$, is larger than the gap between the two DPO seeds ($+0.001$) but under 4% of the value and about $1/200$ of the distance to noise (Table cite86†2 ).\nL231: The directions agree too: the real DPO update overlaps with the swapped control (0.27) almost exactly as much as with a second seed of itself (0.28). Like the SFT update, the DPO update is more concentrated than the pretrained weights and far more than noise, and at the strength of preference learning we reach, almost none of that, in shape or in direction, comes from having learned the preferences.\nL232: The surprise is the shuffled-words control. Text with its word order scrambled, which has little to learn beyond word frequencies, gives the most concentrated update on both models: stable rank 0.042 on Qwen and 0.009 on SmolLM2, with 9% and 21% of the energy in a single direction. This holds in every quarter of the network (Table cite90†12 ). The model fits this data poorly (training loss 5.4, against 2.2 for wikitext; Table cite71†8 ), and we do not know why that gives a more concentrated update.\nL233: One explanation we tested, that the update mainly pushes the output towards frequent tokens, does not single it out: the top directions of the SFT and code updates line up with token frequency in late layers too (Appendix cite43†D ). We report it as an observation: the concentration of a fine-tuning update depends on the training process and the data, not on whether the data teaches instruction following or preferences.\nL234: ### 4.4 Released models\nL235: Figure 3: Released updates (blue) against a Gaussian matrix of the same shape (grey), in three groups: base/instruct releases, the OLMo 2 post-training stages, and code/math specialists. Left: the threshold count (axis starts at 0.85). Right: stable rank. Table 3: Released pairs, means over matrices. “Above floor” is the share of the update’s singular values larger than the largest singular value of the simulated bf16 rounding error: directions that rounding alone cannot produce (§cite7†2 ).\nL236: It is not computed (–) for OLMo 2 base to SFT, whose base, a mid-training checkpoint, is stored in fp32. The last column drops the top direction, to check that one outlier does not drive the result.\nL237: update  | threshold  | noise  | above floor  | stable rank  | noise  | stable w/o top\nL238: SmolLM2-135M instruct  | 0.971  | 0.996  | 96%  | 0.086  | 0.354  | 0.119\nL239: SmolLM2-360M instruct  | 0.964  | 0.996  | 95%  | 0.039  | 0.356  | 0.059\nL240: Qwen2.5-0.5B instruct  | 0.990  | 0.996  | 97%  | 0.280  | 0.434  | 0.314\nL241: Qwen3-0.6B  | 0.989  | 0.996  | 99%  | 0.124  | 0.342  | 0.145\nL242: Qwen2.5-1.5B instruct  | 0.969  | 0.996  | 78%  | 0.051  | 0.432  | 0.073\nL243: SmolLM2-1.7B instruct  | 0.959  | 0.993  | 91%  | 0.075  | 0.336  | 0.090\nL244: Qwen3-8B  | 0.980  | 0.996  | 97%  | 0.082  | 0.373  | 0.098\nL245: OLMo 2 1B: base $\\to$ SFT  | 0.939  | 0.993  | –  | 0.080  | 0.336  | 0.100\nL246: OLMo 2 1B: SFT $\\to$ DPO  | 0.908  | 0.993  | 13%  | 0.012  | 0.336  | 0.023\nL247: OLMo 2 1B: DPO $\\to$ RL  | 0.922  | 0.993  | 3%  | 0.013  | 0.336  | 0.021\nL248: Qwen2.5-Coder-0.5B  | 0.981  | 0.996  | 100%  | 0.194  | 0.434  | 0.228\nL249: Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B  | 0.990  | 0.996  | 100%  | 0.200  | 0.432  | 0.245\nL250: Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B  | 0.987  | 0.996  | 100%  | 0.191  | 0.432  | 0.234\nL251: Released instruct models go through much larger and more varied post-training than our runs. The same pattern holds for all thirteen pairs (Figure cite91†3 , Table cite92†3 ). The threshold count reads 91–99%, close to noise; stable rank puts every update 1.6 to 27 times below noise, and dropping the top direction changes little, so no single outlier drives it. Every update also has tens to hundreds of directions above the largest singular value a same-size Gaussian matrix would have (Table cite93†13 ).\nL252: For most pairs, bf16 rounding is not a concern: 91–100% of their directions are stronger than anything rounding alone can produce. For three it is: 14% of the entries of the Qwen2.5-1.5B update, and 39% and 52% of the OLMo DPO and RL updates, are exactly zero, and only 78%, 13% and 3% of their directions rise above the simulated rounding floor (Tables cite92†3 and cite93†13 ). For these updates much of what the threshold count counts may be rounding.\n"
